.TH HUBLOT 1 "2026" "hublot" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hublot \- terminal UI for a local Docker daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hublot
.RI [ flags ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hublot
is a full-screen terminal interface for the Docker daemon running on the local
machine: live CPU, memory, network and block IO per container, keyboard-driven
navigation into containers, images, volumes, networks and Compose projects, and
every action the Engine API exposes without leaving the interface.
.PP
Two things set it apart from similar tools. It treats disk hygiene as a first
class concern: a view of where disk space went, pruning per object category, and
a preview of exactly what a prune would destroy, reconstructed object by object
because the Engine API offers no dry run. And it is Compose aware: stacks are
rebuilt from the labels Compose writes, grouped by project and service, with
drift detection that tells you whether the running containers still match the
YAML on disk.
.PP
.B hublot
talks to the local daemon socket only. Remote hosts, contexts, TCP and SSH
transports are deliberately out of scope.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.B \-read\-only
Disable every action that changes anything. Destructive keys are greyed out and
a badge appears in the status bar. Use this to look at a server safely.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
Lowercase is safe, uppercase is destructive or forced.
.TP
.B 1..6, tab, shift+tab
Switch view.
.TP
.B /
Incremental filter. Every space-separated term must match.
.TP
.B s, A
Cycle the sort column, reverse the direction.
.TP
.B space, a
Mark the row, mark everything the filter left visible.
.TP
.B enter, l, e
Detail pane, logs, shell inside the container.
.TP
.B S, R, P, K, D
Stop, restart, pause or unpause, kill with a signal, remove.
.TP
.B x
Contextual action palette, searchable by typing. It carries everything without
a key of its own: on a container, rename, cpu and memory limits, processes,
filesystem changes, commit, export, copying files in and out, and attaching or
detaching networks; on an image, tag, untag, save and load; on a volume or a
network, creating one.
.TP
.B t, ?, r, q
Task panel, help, force refresh, quit.
.PP
The help overlay lists every binding and is generated from the same definitions
the program dispatches on, so it cannot fall out of date.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hublot/config.yaml
Optional configuration. Every setting has a working default, so the file may be
absent. Recognised keys: theme, stop_timeout, max_stat_streams, log_tail, shell,
read_only, default_sort, sort_descending, confirm_destructive.
.TP
.I /var/run/docker.sock
The daemon socket of a root installation. If DOCKER_HOST names a unix socket,
that one is used instead; otherwise, when the path above does not exist,
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/docker.sock
is tried, which is where a rootless daemon listens.
.SH PERMISSIONS
.B hublot
enforces nothing of its own: the socket's mode is the access control, exactly as
it is for
.BR docker (1).
A user in the group that owns the socket, usually
.IR docker ,
uses hublot without sudo; anyone else is refused before a single request is
sent, and is told which of the three situations they are in: not in the group,
in it but from a session that started before the membership was granted, or in
it and refused for another reason.
.PP
Being in that group is equivalent to root on the host, since it allows mounting
any path into a container. Running
.B sudo hublot
works as well, with the usual consequences: the configuration read is root's,
under
.IR /root/.config ,
and files written by saving an image or copying out of a container belong to
root.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hublot
exits with 0 when the interface closes normally, and 1 when the daemon cannot be
reached or the configuration file cannot be parsed.
